\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Setup}

We test on synthetic ($N{=}5{,}000$, $K \in \{2, 4\}$, controlled $\Delta\tau$ and $\Delta\pi$, 100 MC runs) and semi-synthetic (TweetEval sentiment~\cite{barbieri2020tweeteval}, $K{=}4$ unbalanced subgroups, $\pi \in \{0.05, 0.10, 0.15, 0.25\}$) DGPs; full specifications in Appendix~\ref{app:dgp}.

We compare: (1) \textit{Naive} (no correction), (2) \textit{Global} (pooled confusion matrix, equivalent to PPI/DSL~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi,egami2024dsl} with homogeneous error), (3) \textit{Stratified MLE} (per-subgroup DSL without shrinkage), (4) \textit{HB EC-HTE} (our method), (5) \textit{PPCI}~\cite{sui2026ppci}, and (6) \textit{EC-HTE+PPCI}.
For CausalForest~\cite{wager2018causal}, we compare naive, global, and EC-HTE variants.
Primary metrics are $|\text{bias}|$, RMSE, and 95\% CI coverage, averaged across subgroups and 100 MC runs.
Robustness validation covers hate speech, six OpenAI models, subgroup misspecification, asymmetric confusion matrices, confounded treatment, and outcome-dependent misclassification (all $\geq$93.5\% coverage; Appendices~\ref{app:hatespeech}--\ref{app:a1_sensitivity}).

\subsection{Empirical Validation of the Bias Identity}
\label{sec:bias_mechanism}

We verify Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} on a $K{=}2$ grid (520 configurations, $\Delta\pi \in [0, 0.25]$, $\Delta\tau \in (0, 0.50]$) and a $K{=}4$ grid (Appendix~\ref{app:dgp}).
Regressing observed subgroup bias on $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1{-}2\bar{\pi})$ yields $R^2 = 0.994$ ($K{=}2$) and $0.974$ ($K{=}4$); residual variance reflects MC noise ($R^2 \geq 0.976$ even at $P(Z{=}1) = 0.1$; Appendix~\ref{app:classimbalance}).
Global correction worsens bias in 50\% of $K{=}2$ configurations (260/520, excluding $\Delta\pi{=}0$), with amplification reaching 2.86$\times$ ($K{=}2$) and 2.41$\times$ ($K{=}4$); Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} visualizes the amplification surface.
Beyond amplification, global correction reverses the sign of the estimated CATE for at least one subgroup in {\bfseries[XX]\%} of configurations ({\bfseries[N/M]}, {\bfseries[ZZ]\%} of all subgroup-rows), with all eight real LLMs exhibiting the same pattern ({\bfseries[YY]\%}); EC-HTE eliminates sign reversal entirely (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_reversal}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig2_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Bias amplification under global correction ($K{=}2$). (a) Low-error subgroup: correction always helps. (b) High-error subgroup: correction \emph{increases} bias when $\Delta\pi > 0.12$ (hatched), reaching 2.86$\times$.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{EC-HTE Evaluation}
\label{sec:ec_hte_eval}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} presents the main results across representative configurations.
In the $K{=}2$ extreme row, global correction \emph{increases} $|\text{bias}|$ relative to naive (0.059 vs.\ 0.058), confirming the over-correction mechanism of Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}.
F-L scalar correction ($\hat{\tau}/(1{-}2\bar{\pi})$) increases $|\text{bias}|$ to 0.122 with coverage 0.68---worse than naive---because a single scalar cannot correct heterogeneous attenuation; F-L per-subgroup inflates bias to 0.168 as $(1{-}2\pi_s) \to 0$ (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}).
HB EC-HTE reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.059 to 0.008 (7.4$\times$) at $K{=}2$ and from 0.056 to 0.012 (4.7$\times$) at $K{=}4$, maintaining near-nominal coverage (0.94--0.96) with lower RMSE than stratified MLE.
Under moderate heterogeneity and in TweetEval ($K{=}4$), methods converge ($p > 0.05$); EC-HTE incurs higher RMSE when $\Delta\pi$ is small (Table~\ref{tab:main_supp}).

\paragraph{CausalForest.}
With CausalForest~\cite{wager2018causal} ($K{=}4$, extreme, $n{=}500$), EC-HTE reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.191 to 0.039 (4.9$\times$) with coverage 0.90; global amplifies bias up to 5.9$\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}; bootstrap SE in Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results (extreme heterogeneity). Metrics averaged over subgroups, $z$-levels, 100 MC runs. Bold: lowest $|\text{bias}|$. $^\dagger$RMSE exceeds Global. F-L Scalar: $\hat{\tau}/(1{-}2\bar{\pi})$~\cite{frazis2003estimating}; F-L Per-sub.: per-subgroup variant. Moderate heterogeneity and TweetEval results in Table~\ref{tab:main_supp}; MC SEs in Appendix~\ref{app:mcse}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{ll ccc}
\toprule
Configuration & Method & $|\text{Bias}|$ & RMSE & 95\% CI Cov. \\
\midrule
\multirow{6}{*}{\shortstack[l]{DIM\\$K{=}2$, $n{=}250$}}
 & Naive & 0.058 & 0.090 & 0.82 \\
 & Global & 0.059 & 0.099 & 0.87 \\
 & F-L Scalar & 0.122 & 0.159 & 0.68 \\
 & F-L Per-sub. & 0.168 & 0.220 & 0.65 \\
 & Strat.\ MLE & 0.012 & 0.094 & 0.95 \\
 & HB EC-HTE & \textbf{0.008} & 0.089 & 0.94 \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{\shortstack[l]{DIM\\$K{=}4$, $n{=}500$}}
 & Naive & 0.062 & 0.113 & 0.88 \\
 & Global & 0.056 & 0.125 & 0.91 \\
 & Strat.\ MLE & \textbf{0.010} & 0.127$^\dagger$ & 0.95 \\
 & HB EC-HTE & 0.012 & 0.120 & 0.96 \\
\midrule
\multirow{3}{*}{\shortstack[l]{CausalForest\\$K{=}4$, $n{=}500$}}
 & Naive & 0.208 & 0.231 & 0.44 \\
 & Global & 0.191 & 0.244 & 0.58 \\
 & EC-HTE Forest & \textbf{0.039} & 0.180 & 0.90 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\subsection{Actionable Diagnostic Without EC-HTE}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} provides a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic: global correction increases bias in subgroup $s$ iff $|\delta_s| / ((1-2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s) > 1$, computable from a pilot sample without estimating treatment effects.
A threshold classifier achieves AUC = 0.86 at the subgroup level (Figure~\ref{fig:roc}, Appendix).
On real LLM confusion matrices (16 combinations; Appendix~\ref{app:screening}), AUC drops to 0.63; restricting to subgroups with $\tau_s > 0$ recovers AUC $= 1.0$.
For $K{=}2$, $|\delta_0|{=}|\delta_1|$ limits discrimination; a meta-criterion ($\max_s \mathrm{score}(s) > 1$) recovers reliability (AUC $\geq 0.75$) for $K{\geq}4$, while $\max_s \mathrm{score}(s) \leq 1$ signals unreliable regimes (Appendix~\ref{app:screening}).

The condition number $\kappa(\hat{C}_s)$ governs correction at three levels: $\kappa > 100$ triggers the singularity guard; $\kappa \leq 1.25$ guarantees strongest bias reduction; $\kappa \in (1.25, 3]$---covering all 13 tested LLM--dataset combinations---maintains calibrated coverage ($\geq$93\% vs.\ Global 86--91\%) with RMSE gains at $n_{\text{expert}} \geq 500$ (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_analysis}).
Budget crossover curves, $K{=}8$ scalability, and multi-LLM validation ($R^2 = 0.975$) appear in Appendices~\ref{sec:budget}--\ref{app:multilm}.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

We derived a bias identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}): global confusion-matrix correction introduces bias proportional to $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau$, reversing CATE signs in {\bfseries[XX]\%} of configurations and all eight real LLMs tested.
EC-HTE eliminates sign reversal via hierarchical Bayesian correction; the diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}; AUC $= 1.0$) flags harmful correction from error rates alone, given sufficient $\Delta\pi$ and expert budget.

